  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Device Registration", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Azure AD module logged a Graph API access-method registration event. data.srcip=194.165.16.77 — an IP that has NO preceding interactive sign-in for alice.jones@corp.com — issued a POST against the phone-authentication-method endpoint and the Azure AD AuditLog recorded activity 'User registered security info — phone method added' for the user alice.jones@corp.com. Wazuh fired custom rule 92310 ('MFA method registered by bearer token — no interactive login') and 92311 ('Graph API: phoneMethods POST from unknown IP'). The hallmark of T1098.005 is present: a second factor was added to an account by a source that never authenticated interactively as that account — consistent with a stolen bearer token harvested from an infostealer log being used to add an attacker-controlled phone.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'registered security info' / 'phone method added' with the target user alice.jones@corp.com and data.srcip=194.165.16.77. rule.mitre.id T1098.005 was attached automatically. The univariate signature: a security-info registration event whose initiator IP has zero matching interactive-logon events in the preceding window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Authenticator Enrollment", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 10:04:55 CEST (~3 minutes after the phone method was added), the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 issued a POST against the Microsoft Authenticator method endpoint for alice.jones@corp.com — registering an attacker-controlled authenticator app as an additional strong second factor. Wazuh fired rule 92310 again ('MFA method registered by bearer token — no interactive login'). Adding a SECOND independent MFA method (phone + authenticator app) is a deliberate redundancy move: even if the victim or helpdesk removes one rogue method, the attacker retains the other. rule.mitre.id T1098.005 was attached.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure', rule.description matched 'authenticator method' / 'registered security info' for user alice.jones@corp.com with data.srcip=194.165.16.77 identical to step 1. The repetition of rule 92310 for a distinct method type within minutes is the multi-method-redundancy signature of an attacker hardening their own persistence.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:04:55+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Software TOTP Seed Registration", "technique": "T1098.005", "what_happened": "At 10:08:40 CEST, the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 issued a POST against the software OATH (TOTP) method endpoint for alice.jones@corp.com, attempting to register an attacker-controlled RFC 6238 software-token seed. Wazuh fired rule 92310 ('MFA method registered by bearer token — no interactive login'). A software TOTP seed the attacker fully controls is the most durable of the three methods — it requires no phone number and no device, only the shared secret, so it survives SIM-swap reversal and device-revocation. rule.mitre.id T1098.005 was attached.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure', rule.description matched 'software OATH' / 'TOTP' / 'registered security info' for user alice.jones@corp.com with the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77. Three distinct method-type registrations (phone, authenticator, software OATH) from one IP inside ~7 minutes is the canonical account-manipulation-for-persistence pattern.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:08:40+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence / Valid Accounts — Post-MFA-Add Sign-In From New Geo", "technique": "T1098.005 + T1078.004", "what_happened": "At 10:14:38 CEST (~12 minutes after the first MFA method was registered), data.srcip=194.165.16.77 produced an interactive sign-in event for alice.jones@corp.com against the IdP host 10.108.57.184 — the FIRST sign-in from this IP for this account, occurring AFTER the rogue MFA methods were registered. Wazuh fired rule 92312 ('MFA add followed by login from new geo within 30min'). The sign-in originates from the same non-corporate IP that registered the MFA methods, in a geography (CIS) inconsistent with the user's normal location. The sequence — bearer-token-only MFA registration, then a fresh interactive sign-in from the same IP satisfying the freshly-added factor — is the definitive close of the T1098.005 loop: the attacker has converted stolen-token access into a fully MFA-satisfying, password-reset-surviving foothold.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92312 fired correlating a security-info registration event for alice.jones@corp.com with a subsequent sign-in event for the same user from the same data.srcip=194.165.16.77 within a 30-minute window. rule.mitre.id T1098.005 + T1078.004 attached. The 'MFA-add-then-login-from-same-external-IP' correlation is the high-fidelity signature distinguishing rogue MFA registration from a benign user enrolling a new device.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:14:38+0200"}
  ],
